  "query_preview": "..A strong ridge is building overhead this morning,\nwith much drier air advecting in from the southwest. Satellite\nderived PWs indicate values in the 0.3 to 0.5 inch range for for\nwestern Utah, with values up to 0.8 inch still hanging out over\nsoutheast Utah. The drying trend will continue through the\nweekend, accelerating later today as the flow gains more of a\nwesterly component. CAMs are still indicating the possibility of a\nfew storms this afternoon, primarily focused over the Uintas, with\nlittle to no potential for convection over the weekend. \n\nHigh temperatures will be noticeably hotter than yesterday, with\nhighs around 5F above seasonal normals for most locations.\nTemperatures will be hot enough to bring an increased potential of\nheat related illness for the southern Utah valleys this afternoon.\nTemperatures will warm further tomorrow as the ridge reaches its\nmaximum strength. Highs up to 10F above normal will likely bring\nheat to dangerous levels for much of the remainder of the valleys\nof Utah. Have added a Heat Advisory for tomorrow for eastern Box\nElder County, with headlines otherwise looking good. Overall,\nHeatRisk values have been trending downward given more reasonable\novernight lows compared to the last heat wave. However, think that\nif anything the high temperature forecast is on the low side, so\nwould not be surprised if red (major) HeatRisk values are more\nwidespread than forecast for Saturday.\n\nA Pacific Northwest trough making its way onshore will shift the\nridge eastward for Sunday. Thus, anticipating northern Utah maxes\nto drop a bit for Sunday as the valleys of southern Utah stay\ndangerously hot. West to southwest winds will increase as the\ntrough moves closer, and breezy conditions over western Utah could\ncombined with very low relative humidity values to bring areas of\ncritical fire weather conditions. The trough is expected to track\nwell north of the area on Monday, but will bring enough less warm\nair to drop maxes for northern portions of the area to near normal\nfor this time of year. Southern Utah maxes (particularly southeast\nUtah maxes), will stay high enough to likely keep heat in the\ndangerous range. \n\nBehind the trough, most guidance puts Utah and southwest Wyoming\nunder a roughly zonal flow aloft. This will likely keep northern\nand central Utah dry, but at least some guidance indicates enough\nof a southerly component to the flow to bring some monsoon\nmoisture back into southern Utah as early as Thursday, bringing an\nincreased threat of showers and thunderstorms.",
